#### 10.3.2 Processing of safety communications
#### 10.3.2.1 Emerging safety issues
An Emerging Safety Issue (ESI) is a safety concern — related to a pharmaceutical product’s
quality, safety or efficacy — considered by a marketing authorization holder (MAH) or identified
by EDA to require urgent attention because of: (a) the potential major impact on the risk-benefit
balance of the product; and/or (b) a significant threat to patients’ or public health; and (c) the
potential need for prompt regulatory action and/or communication to patients and healthcare
professionals. ESIs include, but are not limited to, quality defects with actual or potential clinical
consequences. ESIs shall be distinguished from routine safety updates, which follow standard
PBRER/ICSR reporting timelines.
Examples include; not restricted to:
-     Major safety issues identified in the context of ongoing or newly completed studies, e.g. an
unexpectedly increased rate of fatal or life-threatening adverse events;
-     Major safety issues identified through the spontaneous reporting system or publications in
the scientific literature, which may lead to considering a contraindication, a restriction of use
of a pharmaceutical product or its withdrawal from the market;ific literature, which may lead to considering a contraindication, a restriction of use
of a pharmaceutical product or its withdrawal from the market;

-    Major safety-related regulatory actions outside Egypt, e.g. a restriction of use of a
pharmaceutical product or its suspension.
-    Major safety issues may be foreseen due to shortage or lack of supply of products or raw
materials.
-    Safety issues arising from post-authorization safety studies (PASS) or post-marketing studies
that demonstrate a major unexpected safety concern.
-    Quality defects (e.g., contamination, out-of-specification results, packaging errors) with
confirmed or potential clinical consequences, including patient harm reports.
-    New safety signals from real-world evidence (RWE) sources, national pharmacovigilance
databases, or AI-based signal detection systems that meet ESI criteria.
-    Safety concerns identified during regulatory inspections (GMP, GVP, GCP) with direct
implications for patient safety.ems that meet ESI criteria.
-    Safety concerns identified during regulatory inspections (GMP, GVP, GCP) with direct
implications for patient safety.

These events/observations, which may affect the risk-benefit balance of a pharmaceutical product,
are not to be submitted as ICSRs. They shall be notified as Emerging Safety Issues in writing to
the general administration for pharmaceutical vigilance (PVGA); this shall be done immediately
(no later than 5 working days), once published or implemented in other regulatory authorities, in
which the product is marketed.
The document shall indicate the points of concern and the actions proposed in relation to the
marketing application/authorization for the concerned pharmaceutical product. Those safety issues
shall also be analyzed in the relevant sections of the periodic safety update report of the authorized
In the period between the approval of the pharmacovigilance part of marketing authorization
application and the granting of the marketing authorization, information (quality, non-clinical,
clinical) that could impact on the risk-benefit balance of the pharmaceutical product under
evaluation may become available to the applicant. It is the responsibility of the applicant to ensure
that this information is immediately submitted to EDA.[EDA Document: Guideline_on_Good_Pharmacovigilance_Practice_Egypt_2026.md]

