### 2.3 Hospital Implementation Procedures

**Pharmacy and protocol responsibilities**:
- The pharmacy director identifies pharmacists responsible for preparing the list of antimicrobials used for surgical-site-infection prophylaxis.
- The responsible pharmacists prepare a list of antimicrobials available in the hospital.
- The pharmacists meet with healthcare providers in surgical departments to identify the procedures performed and classify surgical patients.
- The pharmacists develop a protocol that specifies prophylactic antimicrobials and alternatives for different surgical procedures.
- The protocol should include definitions, surgical procedure and wound classification, antimicrobial selection, alternatives, timing, route, dose, frequency, and postoperative duration where applicable.
- The pharmacy director and responsible team present the protocol to relevant committees, including the Drug and Therapeutics Committee and the Antimicrobial Committee, for approval.
- The approved protocol and antimicrobial list are distributed to all surgical departments.
- The pharmacy director, main pharmacy, or medication-supply officer ensures availability of the agreed antimicrobials.d to all surgical departments.
- The pharmacy director, main pharmacy, or medication-supply officer ensures availability of the agreed antimicrobials.

**Responsible stakeholders**:
- Heads of surgical departments and medical teams.
- Drug and Pharmacy Committee.
- Antimicrobial Committee.
- Pharmacy director.
- Clinical pharmacists.
- Quality officer.

**Required forms and records**:
- List of antimicrobials available in the hospital.
- Medication record for antimicrobials included in the protocol.
- Surgical-site-infection prophylaxis antimicrobial protocol.

