This file is designed for direct Markdown ingestion into a RAG pipeline. It preserves the supplied OTC list and converts its tabular content into labeled, searchable text.[Drug: Paracetamol (Acetaminophen)]
Indications / Uses: Relief of mild to moderate pain (headache, muscle aches, toothache, backache, menstrual cramps); reduction of fever.[Drug: Paracetamol (Acetaminophen)]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: 500–1000 mg every 4–6 hours as needed; maximum 4 g/day (reduce to a maximum of 3 g/day with chronic use, low body weight, or hepatic risk factors). Children: weight-based dosing per product label.

(...continued from previous section: "Relief of mild to moderate pain (headache, muscle aches, toothache, backache, menstrual cramps); reduction of fever.")[Drug: Paracetamol (Acetaminophen)]
Contraindications: Severe hepatic impairment or active severe liver disease; known hypersensitivity to paracetamol.

(...continued from previous section: "ts: 500–1000 mg every 4–6 hours as needed; maximum 4 g/day (reduce to a maximum of 3 g/day with chronic use, low body weight, or hepatic risk factors). Children: weight-based dosing per product label.")[Drug: Paracetamol (Acetaminophen)]
Warnings & Precautions: Hepatotoxicity is the principal risk, occurring with acute overdose or with chronic supratherapeutic dosing (including combined use of multiple paracetamol-containing products). Use caution with chronic alcohol use, malnutrition, or pre-existing liver disease. Rare but serious skin reactions (Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis) have been reported.

(...continued from previous section: "Severe hepatic impairment or active severe liver disease; known hypersensitivity to paracetamol.")[Drug: Paracetamol (Acetaminophen)]
Common Side Effects: Generally well tolerated at recommended doses; occasional nausea.

(...continued from previous section: "products). Use caution with chronic alcohol use, malnutrition, or pre-existing liver disease. Rare but serious skin reactions (Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis) have been reported.")[Drug: Paracetamol (Acetaminophen)]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Hepatotoxicity/acute liver failure (overdose); rare severe cutaneous adverse reactions.

(...continued from previous section: "Generally well tolerated at recommended doses; occasional nausea.")[Drug: Paracetamol (Acetaminophen)]
Drug Interactions: Chronic use may potentiate the effect of warfarin (increased INR) — monitor with regular use; chronic alcohol use increases hepatotoxicity risk.

(...continued from previous section: "Hepatotoxicity/acute liver failure (overdose); rare severe cutaneous adverse reactions.")[Drug: Paracetamol (Acetaminophen)]
Use in Pregnancy: Considered the preferred analgesic/antipyretic in pregnancy at therapeutic doses for the shortest duration needed.

(...continued from previous section: "Chronic use may potentiate the effect of warfarin (increased INR) — monitor with regular use; chronic alcohol use increases hepatotoxicity risk.")[Drug: Paracetamol (Acetaminophen)]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Compatible with breastfeeding at recommended doses.

(...continued from previous section: "Considered the preferred analgesic/antipyretic in pregnancy at therapeutic doses for the shortest duration needed.")[Drug: Paracetamol (Acetaminophen)]
Storage Instructions: Store below 30°C (room temperature), protect from moisture; keep suppositories away from heat.

(...continued from previous section: "Compatible with breastfeeding at recommended doses.")[Drug: Ibuprofen]
Indications / Uses: Relief of mild to moderate pain, fever, dysmenorrhea, and signs/symptoms of osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis.[Drug: Ibuprofen]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: 200–400 mg every 4–6 hours as needed; prescription regimens up to 1200–3200 mg/day in divided doses for inflammatory conditions. Take with food or milk if GI upset occurs.

(...continued from previous section: "Relief of mild to moderate pain, fever, dysmenorrhea, and signs/symptoms of osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis.")[Drug: Ibuprofen]
Contraindications: Known NSAID/aspirin hypersensitivity (asthma, urticaria, angioedema); active GI bleeding/ulceration; setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery; third trimester of pregnancy.

(...continued from previous section: "Adults: 200–400 mg every 4–6 hours as needed; prescription regimens up to 1200–3200 mg/day in divided doses for inflammatory conditions. Take with food or milk if GI upset occurs.")[Drug: Ibuprofen]
Warnings & Precautions: Boxed-warning-level risks: increased risk of serious cardiovascular thrombotic events (MI, stroke) and serious GI adverse events (bleeding, ulceration, perforation), which can occur without warning and be fatal. Risk increases with dose, duration, and in the elderly. Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration.

(...continued from previous section: "Known NSAID/aspirin hypersensitivity (asthma, urticaria, angioedema); active GI bleeding/ulceration; setting of coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery; third trimester of pregnancy.")[Drug: Ibuprofen]
Common Side Effects: Dyspepsia, heartburn, nausea, headache, dizziness.

(...continued from previous section: "ents (bleeding, ulceration, perforation), which can occur without warning and be fatal. Risk increases with dose, duration, and in the elderly. Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration.")[Drug: Ibuprofen]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: GI bleeding/perforation, myocardial infarction, stroke, hypertension, fluid retention/heart failure, renal impairment.

(...continued from previous section: "Dyspepsia, heartburn, nausea, headache, dizziness.")[Drug: Ibuprofen]
Drug Interactions: Aspirin and other NSAIDs (increased GI bleeding risk); anticoagulants (bleeding risk); ACE inhibitors/ARBs and diuretics (reduced antihypertensive effect, renal risk); lithium and methotrexate (increased levels).

(...continued from previous section: "GI bleeding/perforation, myocardial infarction, stroke, hypertension, fluid retention/heart failure, renal impairment.")[Drug: Ibuprofen]
Use in Pregnancy: Avoid, particularly from 20 weeks and in the third trimester (risk of fetal renal dysfunction and premature ductus arteriosus closure).

(...continued from previous section: "other NSAIDs (increased GI bleeding risk); anticoagulants (bleeding risk); ACE inhibitors/ARBs and diuretics (reduced antihypertensive effect, renal risk); lithium and methotrexate (increased levels).")[Drug: Ibuprofen]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Generally considered compatible in short-term, low-dose use; discuss with a physician.

(...continued from previous section: "Avoid, particularly from 20 weeks and in the third trimester (risk of fetal renal dysfunction and premature ductus arteriosus closure).")[Drug: Ibuprofen]
Storage Instructions: Store at room temperature (20–25°C), protect from moisture.

(...continued from previous section: "Generally considered compatible in short-term, low-dose use; discuss with a physician.")[Drug: Diclofenac]
Indications / Uses: Osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, acute musculoskeletal pain, dysmenorrhea, post-operative pain.[Drug: Diclofenac]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: 50 mg 2–3 times daily (max 150 mg/day), or 75–100 mg/day sustained-release; IM injection 75 mg once or twice daily for short-term use.

(...continued from previous section: "Osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, acute musculoskeletal pain, dysmenorrhea, post-operative pain.")[Drug: Diclofenac]
Contraindications: Active peptic ulcer/GI bleeding; severe heart failure; setting of CABG surgery; known hypersensitivity to aspirin/NSAIDs; third trimester of pregnancy.

(...continued from previous section: "Adults: 50 mg 2–3 times daily (max 150 mg/day), or 75–100 mg/day sustained-release; IM injection 75 mg once or twice daily for short-term use.")[Drug: Diclofenac]
Warnings & Precautions: Same class warnings as other NSAIDs: increased cardiovascular thrombotic risk and serious GI bleeding risk; caution with renal or hepatic impairment; monitor liver enzymes with prolonged use.

(...continued from previous section: "Active peptic ulcer/GI bleeding; severe heart failure; setting of CABG surgery; known hypersensitivity to aspirin/NSAIDs; third trimester of pregnancy.")[Drug: Diclofenac]
Common Side Effects: Dyspepsia, headache, dizziness, elevated liver enzymes.

(...continued from previous section: "Same class warnings as other NSAIDs: increased cardiovascular thrombotic risk and serious GI bleeding risk; caution with renal or hepatic impairment; monitor liver enzymes with prolonged use.")[Drug: Diclofenac]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: GI bleeding/perforation, myocardial infarction/stroke, acute renal failure, hepatotoxicity.

(...continued from previous section: "Dyspepsia, headache, dizziness, elevated liver enzymes.")[Drug: Diclofenac]
Drug Interactions: Other NSAIDs/aspirin, anticoagulants, ACE inhibitors/ARBs/diuretics (reduced effect and renal risk), lithium, methotrexate, cyclosporine.

(...continued from previous section: "GI bleeding/perforation, myocardial infarction/stroke, acute renal failure, hepatotoxicity.")[Drug: Diclofenac]
Use in Pregnancy: Avoid, especially from 20 weeks onward and in the third trimester.

(...continued from previous section: "Other NSAIDs/aspirin, anticoagulants, ACE inhibitors/ARBs/diuretics (reduced effect and renal risk), lithium, methotrexate, cyclosporine.")[Drug: Diclofenac]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Use only if clearly needed; short courses generally considered lower risk.

(...continued from previous section: "Avoid, especially from 20 weeks onward and in the third trimester.")[Drug: Diclofenac]
Storage Instructions: Store below 25–30°C, protect from light; do not refrigerate suppositories unless specified.

(...continued from previous section: "Use only if clearly needed; short courses generally considered lower risk.")[Drug: Aspirin (Acetylsalicylic Acid)]
Indications / Uses: Low dose (75–150 mg/day): antiplatelet therapy for secondary prevention of cardiovascular/cerebrovascular events. Higher dose: relief of mild to moderate pain and fever, anti-inflammatory use.[Drug: Aspirin (Acetylsalicylic Acid)]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Cardioprotective: 75–100 mg once daily. Analgesic/antipyretic: 325–650 mg every 4–6 hours as needed (adult use only).

(...continued from previous section: "Low dose (75–150 mg/day): antiplatelet therapy for secondary prevention of cardiovascular/cerebrovascular events. Higher dose: relief of mild to moderate pain and fever, anti-inflammatory use.")[Drug: Aspirin (Acetylsalicylic Acid)]
Contraindications: Active peptic ulcer or GI bleeding; bleeding disorders; known aspirin/NSAID hypersensitivity; children and teenagers with viral illness (risk of Reye's syndrome); third trimester of pregnancy at analgesic doses.

(...continued from previous section: "Cardioprotective: 75–100 mg once daily. Analgesic/antipyretic: 325–650 mg every 4–6 hours as needed (adult use only).")[Drug: Aspirin (Acetylsalicylic Acid)]
Warnings & Precautions: GI bleeding risk increases with dose, age, and concomitant anticoagulant/NSAID use. Reye's syndrome risk in children/adolescents with viral infections — aspirin is not used for fever in this population. Increased bleeding risk with surgery/dental procedures.

(...continued from previous section: "ic ulcer or GI bleeding; bleeding disorders; known aspirin/NSAID hypersensitivity; children and teenagers with viral illness (risk of Reye's syndrome); third trimester of pregnancy at analgesic doses.")[Drug: Aspirin (Acetylsalicylic Acid)]
Common Side Effects: Dyspepsia, heartburn, easy bruising.

(...continued from previous section: " anticoagulant/NSAID use. Reye's syndrome risk in children/adolescents with viral infections — aspirin is not used for fever in this population. Increased bleeding risk with surgery/dental procedures.")[Drug: Aspirin (Acetylsalicylic Acid)]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: GI bleeding, hemorrhagic stroke, tinnitus/hearing changes (sign of toxicity), hypersensitivity reactions.

(...continued from previous section: "Dyspepsia, heartburn, easy bruising.")[Drug: Aspirin (Acetylsalicylic Acid)]
Drug Interactions: Other NSAIDs and anticoagulants (additive bleeding risk); methotrexate (increased toxicity); ACE inhibitors (reduced effect); uricosuric agents (reduced effect at low dose).

(...continued from previous section: "GI bleeding, hemorrhagic stroke, tinnitus/hearing changes (sign of toxicity), hypersensitivity reactions.")[Drug: Aspirin (Acetylsalicylic Acid)]
Use in Pregnancy: Low-dose aspirin may be used under medical supervision for specific indications (e.g., pre-eclampsia risk reduction); avoid regular analgesic-dose use, especially in the third trimester.

(...continued from previous section: "Other NSAIDs and anticoagulants (additive bleeding risk); methotrexate (increased toxicity); ACE inhibitors (reduced effect); uricosuric agents (reduced effect at low dose).")[Drug: Aspirin (Acetylsalicylic Acid)]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Avoid regular use; occasional low-dose use is generally considered lower risk — consult a physician.

(...continued from previous section: "Low-dose aspirin may be used under medical supervision for specific indications (e.g., pre-eclampsia risk reduction); avoid regular analgesic-dose use, especially in the third trimester.")[Drug: Aspirin (Acetylsalicylic Acid)]
Storage Instructions: Store at room temperature, protect from moisture (tablets can develop a vinegar-like odor when degraded — discard).

(...continued from previous section: "Avoid regular use; occasional low-dose use is generally considered lower risk — consult a physician.")[Drug: Amoxicillin]
Indications / Uses: Respiratory tract infections, ENT infections (otitis media, sinusitis, tonsillitis), urinary tract infections, skin/soft-tissue infections, H. pylori eradication (combination therapy).[Drug: Amoxicillin]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: 250–500 mg every 8 hours, or 500–875 mg every 12 hours depending on severity; children: weight-based dosing.

(...continued from previous section: "Respiratory tract infections, ENT infections (otitis media, sinusitis, tonsillitis), urinary tract infections, skin/soft-tissue infections, H. pylori eradication (combination therapy).")[Drug: Amoxicillin]
Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to penicillins; history of severe penicillin-associated cholestatic jaundice/hepatic dysfunction.

(...continued from previous section: "Adults: 250–500 mg every 8 hours, or 500–875 mg every 12 hours depending on severity; children: weight-based dosing.")[Drug: Amoxicillin]
Warnings & Precautions: Risk of anaphylaxis/severe hypersensitivity in penicillin-allergic patients; Clostridioides difficile-associated diarrhea can occur during or after treatment; use caution in infectious mononucleosis (increased rash incidence).

(...continued from previous section: "Known hypersensitivity to penicillins; history of severe penicillin-associated cholestatic jaundice/hepatic dysfunction.")[Drug: Amoxicillin]
Common Side Effects: Diarrhea, nausea, skin rash.

(...continued from previous section: " hypersensitivity in penicillin-allergic patients; Clostridioides difficile-associated diarrhea can occur during or after treatment; use caution in infectious mononucleosis (increased rash incidence).")[Drug: Amoxicillin]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Anaphylaxis, severe skin reactions, C. difficile colitis.

(...continued from previous section: "Diarrhea, nausea, skin rash.")[Drug: Amoxicillin]
Drug Interactions: Probenecid (increases amoxicillin levels); oral anticoagulants (may increase INR); allopurinol (increased rash incidence).

(...continued from previous section: "Anaphylaxis, severe skin reactions, C. difficile colitis.")[Drug: Amoxicillin]
Use in Pregnancy: Generally considered compatible; commonly used when a penicillin is indicated in pregnancy.

(...continued from previous section: "Probenecid (increases amoxicillin levels); oral anticoagulants (may increase INR); allopurinol (increased rash incidence).")[Drug: Amoxicillin]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Compatible with breastfeeding.

(...continued from previous section: "Generally considered compatible; commonly used when a penicillin is indicated in pregnancy.")[Drug: Amoxicillin]
Storage Instructions: Capsules/tablets: room temperature, protect from moisture. Reconstituted suspension: refrigerate and discard any unused portion after 14 days.

(...continued from previous section: "Compatible with breastfeeding.")[Drug: Co-amoxiclav (Amoxicillin/Clavulanic Acid)]
Indications / Uses: Respiratory and ENT infections, UTIs, skin/soft-tissue infections, and infections caused by beta-lactamase-producing organisms resistant to amoxicillin alone.[Drug: Co-amoxiclav (Amoxicillin/Clavulanic Acid)]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: 625 mg every 8 hours or 1 g every 12 hours, depending on severity and indication.

(...continued from previous section: "Respiratory and ENT infections, UTIs, skin/soft-tissue infections, and infections caused by beta-lactamase-producing organisms resistant to amoxicillin alone.")[Drug: Co-amoxiclav (Amoxicillin/Clavulanic Acid)]
Contraindications: Penicillin hypersensitivity; history of amoxicillin/clavulanate-associated jaundice or hepatic dysfunction.

(...continued from previous section: "Adults: 625 mg every 8 hours or 1 g every 12 hours, depending on severity and indication.")[Drug: Co-amoxiclav (Amoxicillin/Clavulanic Acid)]
Warnings & Precautions: Hepatic dysfunction (including cholestatic jaundice) is reported more frequently than with amoxicillin alone, more common in older adults and with longer treatment courses; hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis risk; C. difficile-associated diarrhea.

(...continued from previous section: "Penicillin hypersensitivity; history of amoxicillin/clavulanate-associated jaundice or hepatic dysfunction.")[Drug: Co-amoxiclav (Amoxicillin/Clavulanic Acid)]
Common Side Effects: Diarrhea, nausea, rash.

(...continued from previous section: "c jaundice) is reported more frequently than with amoxicillin alone, more common in older adults and with longer treatment courses; hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis risk; C. difficile-associated diarrhea.")[Drug: Co-amoxiclav (Amoxicillin/Clavulanic Acid)]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Hepatotoxicity/cholestatic jaundice, anaphylaxis, severe skin reactions, C. difficile colitis.

(...continued from previous section: "Diarrhea, nausea, rash.")[Drug: Co-amoxiclav (Amoxicillin/Clavulanic Acid)]
Drug Interactions: Probenecid (increases levels); oral anticoagulants (increased INR); allopurinol (increased rash risk).

(...continued from previous section: "Hepatotoxicity/cholestatic jaundice, anaphylaxis, severe skin reactions, C. difficile colitis.")[Drug: Co-amoxiclav (Amoxicillin/Clavulanic Acid)]
Use in Pregnancy: Generally considered compatible when a penicillin is indicated.

(...continued from previous section: "Probenecid (increases levels); oral anticoagulants (increased INR); allopurinol (increased rash risk).")[Drug: Co-amoxiclav (Amoxicillin/Clavulanic Acid)]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Compatible with breastfeeding; monitor infant for diarrhea/thrush.

(...continued from previous section: "Generally considered compatible when a penicillin is indicated.")[Drug: Co-amoxiclav (Amoxicillin/Clavulanic Acid)]
Storage Instructions: Room temperature, protect from moisture. Reconstituted suspension: refrigerate, discard after 7–10 days (check product label).

(...continued from previous section: "Compatible with breastfeeding; monitor infant for diarrhea/thrush.")[Drug: Azithromycin]
Indications / Uses: Respiratory tract infections, ENT infections, uncomplicated skin infections, and selected sexually transmitted infections (e.g., chlamydia).[Drug: Azithromycin]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Typical adult course: 500 mg on day 1, then 250 mg once daily on days 2–5, OR 500 mg once daily for 3 days, depending on indication.

(...continued from previous section: "Respiratory tract infections, ENT infections, uncomplicated skin infections, and selected sexually transmitted infections (e.g., chlamydia).")[Drug: Azithromycin]
Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to macrolide antibiotics; history of cholestatic jaundice/hepatic dysfunction associated with prior azithromycin use.

(...continued from previous section: "Typical adult course: 500 mg on day 1, then 250 mg once daily on days 2–5, OR 500 mg once daily for 3 days, depending on indication.")[Drug: Azithromycin]
Warnings & Precautions: Can prolong the QT interval — use caution with other QT-prolonging drugs, electrolyte abnormalities, or known arrhythmia; rare hepatotoxicity; caution in myasthenia gravis (may worsen symptoms).

(...continued from previous section: "Known hypersensitivity to macrolide antibiotics; history of cholestatic jaundice/hepatic dysfunction associated with prior azithromycin use.")[Drug: Azithromycin]
Common Side Effects: Diarrhea, nausea, abdominal pain.

(...continued from previous section: "Can prolong the QT interval — use caution with other QT-prolonging drugs, electrolyte abnormalities, or known arrhythmia; rare hepatotoxicity; caution in myasthenia gravis (may worsen symptoms).")[Drug: Azithromycin]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: QT prolongation/arrhythmia, hepatotoxicity, hypersensitivity reactions.

(...continued from previous section: "Diarrhea, nausea, abdominal pain.")[Drug: Azithromycin]
Drug Interactions: Other QT-prolonging drugs (antiarrhythmics, certain antipsychotics); antacids containing aluminum/magnesium reduce absorption (separate dosing); warfarin (may increase anticoagulant effect).

(...continued from previous section: "QT prolongation/arrhythmia, hepatotoxicity, hypersensitivity reactions.")[Drug: Azithromycin]
Use in Pregnancy: Used when a macrolide is clinically indicated and benefit outweighs risk.

(...continued from previous section: "Other QT-prolonging drugs (antiarrhythmics, certain antipsychotics); antacids containing aluminum/magnesium reduce absorption (separate dosing); warfarin (may increase anticoagulant effect).")[Drug: Azithromycin]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Generally considered compatible; monitor infant for GI effects.

(...continued from previous section: "Used when a macrolide is clinically indicated and benefit outweighs risk.")[Drug: Azithromycin]
Storage Instructions: Tablets/capsules: room temperature. Reconstituted suspension: does not require refrigeration; discard after 10 days (check label).

(...continued from previous section: "Generally considered compatible; monitor infant for GI effects.")[Drug: Ciprofloxacin]
Indications / Uses: Complicated/uncomplicated urinary tract infections, certain gastrointestinal infections, bone and joint infections, and other infections where fluoroquinolone therapy is specifically indicated.[Drug: Ciprofloxacin]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: 250–750 mg every 12 hours depending on indication and severity.

(...continued from previous section: "Complicated/uncomplicated urinary tract infections, certain gastrointestinal infections, bone and joint infections, and other infections where fluoroquinolone therapy is specifically indicated.")[Drug: Ciprofloxacin]
Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to fluoroquinolones; history of tendon disorders related to fluoroquinolone use; avoid in children and during pregnancy unless no alternative exists.

(...continued from previous section: "Adults: 250–750 mg every 12 hours depending on indication and severity.")[Drug: Ciprofloxacin]
Warnings & Precautions: Class warnings include risk of tendonitis and tendon rupture (higher in the elderly, with corticosteroid use, or organ transplant recipients), peripheral neuropathy, CNS effects (seizures, confusion), QT prolongation, and risk of aortic aneurysm/dissection. Reserve for infections without safer alternative options when possible.

(...continued from previous section: "Known hypersensitivity to fluoroquinolones; history of tendon disorders related to fluoroquinolone use; avoid in children and during pregnancy unless no alternative exists.")[Drug: Ciprofloxacin]
Common Side Effects: Nausea, diarrhea, headache, dizziness.

(...continued from previous section: " recipients), peripheral neuropathy, CNS effects (seizures, confusion), QT prolongation, and risk of aortic aneurysm/dissection. Reserve for infections without safer alternative options when possible.")[Drug: Ciprofloxacin]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Tendon rupture, peripheral neuropathy, CNS effects/seizures, C. difficile colitis, hypersensitivity.

(...continued from previous section: "Nausea, diarrhea, headache, dizziness.")[Drug: Ciprofloxacin]
Drug Interactions: Antacids, sucralfate, and products containing calcium/iron/zinc reduce absorption (separate dosing by several hours); increases theophylline levels; may potentiate warfarin effect; NSAIDs may increase seizure risk.

(...continued from previous section: "Tendon rupture, peripheral neuropathy, CNS effects/seizures, C. difficile colitis, hypersensitivity.")[Drug: Ciprofloxacin]
Use in Pregnancy: Avoid unless no alternative therapy is suitable (potential effect on developing cartilage).

(...continued from previous section: "alfate, and products containing calcium/iron/zinc reduce absorption (separate dosing by several hours); increases theophylline levels; may potentiate warfarin effect; NSAIDs may increase seizure risk.")[Drug: Ciprofloxacin]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Generally avoided if alternatives exist; discuss with a physician.

(...continued from previous section: "Avoid unless no alternative therapy is suitable (potential effect on developing cartilage).")[Drug: Ciprofloxacin]
Storage Instructions: Store at room temperature, protect from light.

(...continued from previous section: "Generally avoided if alternatives exist; discuss with a physician.")[Drug: Metronidazole]
Indications / Uses: Anaerobic bacterial infections, protozoal infections (giardiasis, amoebiasis, trichomoniasis), bacterial vaginosis, and as part of combination therapy for H. pylori.[Drug: Metronidazole]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: 400–500 mg 2–3 times daily depending on indication and duration (course length varies by infection type).

(...continued from previous section: "Anaerobic bacterial infections, protozoal infections (giardiasis, amoebiasis, trichomoniasis), bacterial vaginosis, and as part of combination therapy for H. pylori.")[Drug: Metronidazole]
Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to nitroimidazole derivatives; first trimester of pregnancy for non-essential indications.

(...continued from previous section: "Adults: 400–500 mg 2–3 times daily depending on indication and duration (course length varies by infection type).")[Drug: Metronidazole]
Warnings & Precautions: Disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol (flushing, nausea, palpitations) — avoid alcohol during treatment and for at least 48 hours after the last dose; peripheral neuropathy and CNS effects (rare, more common with prolonged/high-dose use); darkening of urine can occur (harmless).

(...continued from previous section: "Known hypersensitivity to nitroimidazole derivatives; first trimester of pregnancy for non-essential indications.")[Drug: Metronidazole]
Common Side Effects: Metallic taste, nausea, dry mouth.

(...continued from previous section: "alcohol during treatment and for at least 48 hours after the last dose; peripheral neuropathy and CNS effects (rare, more common with prolonged/high-dose use); darkening of urine can occur (harmless).")[Drug: Metronidazole]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Peripheral neuropathy, seizures (rare), severe hypersensitivity.

(...continued from previous section: "Metallic taste, nausea, dry mouth.")[Drug: Metronidazole]
Drug Interactions: Alcohol (disulfiram-like reaction); warfarin (increased INR/bleeding risk); lithium (increased levels).

(...continued from previous section: "Peripheral neuropathy, seizures (rare), severe hypersensitivity.")[Drug: Metronidazole]
Use in Pregnancy: Avoid in the first trimester unless clearly needed; may be used later in pregnancy for specific indications under medical guidance.

(...continued from previous section: "Alcohol (disulfiram-like reaction); warfarin (increased INR/bleeding risk); lithium (increased levels).")[Drug: Metronidazole]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Short courses generally considered compatible; high-dose/prolonged use — consult a physician.

(...continued from previous section: "Avoid in the first trimester unless clearly needed; may be used later in pregnancy for specific indications under medical guidance.")[Drug: Metronidazole]
Storage Instructions: Room temperature, protect from light.

(...continued from previous section: "Short courses generally considered compatible; high-dose/prolonged use — consult a physician.")[Drug: Cefixime]
Indications / Uses: Uncomplicated urinary tract infections, respiratory tract infections, and ENT infections caused by susceptible organisms.[Drug: Cefixime]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: 400 mg once daily or 200 mg every 12 hours.

(...continued from previous section: "Uncomplicated urinary tract infections, respiratory tract infections, and ENT infections caused by susceptible organisms.")[Drug: Cefixime]
Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to cephalosporins.

(...continued from previous section: "Adults: 400 mg once daily or 200 mg every 12 hours.")[Drug: Cefixime]
Warnings & Precautions: Cross-reactivity possible in patients with penicillin allergy — use caution; C. difficile-associated diarrhea can occur.

(...continued from previous section: "Known hypersensitivity to cephalosporins.")[Drug: Cefixime]
Common Side Effects: Diarrhea, nausea, abdominal pain.

(...continued from previous section: "Cross-reactivity possible in patients with penicillin allergy — use caution; C. difficile-associated diarrhea can occur.")[Drug: Cefixime]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Anaphylaxis, C. difficile colitis, hepatic enzyme elevation.

(...continued from previous section: "Diarrhea, nausea, abdominal pain.")[Drug: Cefixime]
Drug Interactions: Increases warfarin effect (monitor INR); probenecid increases cefixime levels.

(...continued from previous section: "Anaphylaxis, C. difficile colitis, hepatic enzyme elevation.")[Drug: Cefixime]
Use in Pregnancy: Generally considered compatible when a cephalosporin is indicated.

(...continued from previous section: "Increases warfarin effect (monitor INR); probenecid increases cefixime levels.")[Drug: Cefixime]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Generally considered compatible.

(...continued from previous section: "Generally considered compatible when a cephalosporin is indicated.")[Drug: Cefixime]
Storage Instructions: Room temperature. Reconstituted suspension: store per label, generally refrigerated and discarded after 14 days.

(...continued from previous section: "Generally considered compatible.")[Drug: Metformin]
Indications / Uses: First-line treatment for type 2 diabetes mellitus, alone or in combination with other antidiabetic agents.[Drug: Metformin]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Start 500 mg once or twice daily with meals; titrate gradually to a usual maintenance dose of 1500–2000 mg/day (maximum ~2550 mg/day) to reduce GI side effects.

(...continued from previous section: "First-line treatment for type 2 diabetes mellitus, alone or in combination with other antidiabetic agents.")[Drug: Metformin]
Contraindications: Severe renal impairment (eGFR <30 mL/min/1.73m²); acute or chronic metabolic acidosis, including diabetic ketoacidosis; conditions predisposing to tissue hypoxia (severe infection, shock).

(...continued from previous section: "Start 500 mg once or twice daily with meals; titrate gradually to a usual maintenance dose of 1500–2000 mg/day (maximum ~2550 mg/day) to reduce GI side effects.")[Drug: Metformin]
Warnings & Precautions: Boxed-warning-level risk of lactic acidosis (rare but potentially fatal), increased with renal impairment, dehydration, sepsis, or excessive alcohol intake. Temporarily discontinue before procedures using iodinated contrast media and restart once renal function is confirmed stable. Long-term use may reduce vitamin B12 absorption.

(...continued from previous section: "Severe renal impairment (eGFR <30 mL/min/1.73m²); acute or chronic metabolic acidosis, including diabetic ketoacidosis; conditions predisposing to tissue hypoxia (severe infection, shock).")[Drug: Metformin]
Common Side Effects: Diarrhea, nausea, abdominal discomfort, metallic taste (usually transient, minimized by titration and taking with food).

(...continued from previous section: "excessive alcohol intake. Temporarily discontinue before procedures using iodinated contrast media and restart once renal function is confirmed stable. Long-term use may reduce vitamin B12 absorption.")[Drug: Metformin]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Lactic acidosis (rare); vitamin B12 deficiency with chronic use.

(...continued from previous section: "Diarrhea, nausea, abdominal discomfort, metallic taste (usually transient, minimized by titration and taking with food).")[Drug: Metformin]
Drug Interactions: Iodinated contrast media (hold metformin around procedure); alcohol (increases lactic acidosis risk); carbonic anhydrase inhibitors.

(...continued from previous section: "Lactic acidosis (rare); vitamin B12 deficiency with chronic use.")[Drug: Metformin]
Use in Pregnancy: Used in some cases of gestational diabetes under medical supervision.

(...continued from previous section: "Iodinated contrast media (hold metformin around procedure); alcohol (increases lactic acidosis risk); carbonic anhydrase inhibitors.")[Drug: Metformin]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Considered compatible with breastfeeding.

(...continued from previous section: "Used in some cases of gestational diabetes under medical supervision.")[Drug: Metformin]
Storage Instructions: 

(...continued from previous section: "Considered compatible with breastfeeding.")[Drug: Gliclazide]
Indications / Uses: Type 2 diabetes mellitus, when diet, exercise, and weight loss alone are insufficient.[Drug: Gliclazide]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Modified-release: 30–120 mg once daily with breakfast, titrated based on glycemic response.

(...continued from previous section: "Type 2 diabetes mellitus, when diet, exercise, and weight loss alone are insufficient.")[Drug: Gliclazide]
Contraindications: Type 1 diabetes/diabetic ketoacidosis; severe hepatic or renal impairment; known hypersensitivity to sulfonylureas or sulfonamides.

(...continued from previous section: "Modified-release: 30–120 mg once daily with breakfast, titrated based on glycemic response.")[Drug: Gliclazide]
Warnings & Precautions: Risk of hypoglycemia, particularly in the elderly, with irregular meals, renal/hepatic impairment, or alcohol use; weight gain is common; counsel patients on recognizing and managing hypoglycemia.

(...continued from previous section: "Type 1 diabetes/diabetic ketoacidosis; severe hepatic or renal impairment; known hypersensitivity to sulfonylureas or sulfonamides.")[Drug: Gliclazide]
Common Side Effects: Hypoglycemia, mild GI upset.

(...continued from previous section: "Risk of hypoglycemia, particularly in the elderly, with irregular meals, renal/hepatic impairment, or alcohol use; weight gain is common; counsel patients on recognizing and managing hypoglycemia.")[Drug: Gliclazide]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Severe/prolonged hypoglycemia, rare hepatic dysfunction.

(...continued from previous section: "Hypoglycemia, mild GI upset.")[Drug: Gliclazide]
Drug Interactions: Other glucose-lowering agents (additive hypoglycemia); beta-blockers (may mask hypoglycemia symptoms); NSAIDs and certain antibiotics may potentiate hypoglycemic effect; alcohol.

(...continued from previous section: "Severe/prolonged hypoglycemia, rare hepatic dysfunction.")[Drug: Gliclazide]
Use in Pregnancy: Not recommended; insulin is preferred for glycemic control in pregnancy.

(...continued from previous section: "Other glucose-lowering agents (additive hypoglycemia); beta-blockers (may mask hypoglycemia symptoms); NSAIDs and certain antibiotics may potentiate hypoglycemic effect; alcohol.")[Drug: Gliclazide]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Not recommended; consult a physician for alternatives.

(...continued from previous section: "Not recommended; insulin is preferred for glycemic control in pregnancy.")[Drug: Gliclazide]
Storage Instructions: 

(...continued from previous section: "Not recommended; consult a physician for alternatives.")[Drug: Insulin (Regular / NPH / Analogue formulations)]
Indications / Uses: Type 1 diabetes mellitus (essential); type 2 diabetes mellitus inadequately controlled on oral agents; gestational diabetes; diabetic emergencies (e.g., DKA, with regular/rapid-acting insulin).[Drug: Insulin (Regular / NPH / Analogue formulations)]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Individualized based on blood glucose monitoring, body weight, meal patterns, and concurrent therapy; regimens vary widely (basal-bolus, premixed, basal-only).

(...continued from previous section: "Type 1 diabetes mellitus (essential); type 2 diabetes mellitus inadequately controlled on oral agents; gestational diabetes; diabetic emergencies (e.g., DKA, with regular/rapid-acting insulin).")[Drug: Insulin (Regular / NPH / Analogue formulations)]
Contraindications: Episodes of hypoglycemia; known hypersensitivity to the specific insulin product or its excipients.

(...continued from previous section: "Individualized based on blood glucose monitoring, body weight, meal patterns, and concurrent therapy; regimens vary widely (basal-bolus, premixed, basal-only).")[Drug: Insulin (Regular / NPH / Analogue formulations)]
Warnings & Precautions: Hypoglycemia is the principal risk — patients and caregivers should be trained to recognize and treat it; do not interchange insulin products/concentrations without medical guidance (dosing errors can be serious); injection site rotation is needed to prevent lipodystrophy, which can affect absorption.

(...continued from previous section: "Episodes of hypoglycemia; known hypersensitivity to the specific insulin product or its excipients.")[Drug: Insulin (Regular / NPH / Analogue formulations)]
Common Side Effects: Hypoglycemia, injection site reactions, weight gain.

(...continued from previous section: "it; do not interchange insulin products/concentrations without medical guidance (dosing errors can be serious); injection site rotation is needed to prevent lipodystrophy, which can affect absorption.")[Drug: Insulin (Regular / NPH / Analogue formulations)]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Severe hypoglycemia (confusion, seizure, loss of consciousness), rare severe allergic reactions, lipodystrophy at injection sites.

(...continued from previous section: "Hypoglycemia, injection site reactions, weight gain.")[Drug: Insulin (Regular / NPH / Analogue formulations)]
Drug Interactions: Other glucose-lowering drugs (additive hypoglycemia risk); beta-blockers (may mask hypoglycemia symptoms); corticosteroids and some diuretics can raise glucose and increase insulin requirements.

(...continued from previous section: "Severe hypoglycemia (confusion, seizure, loss of consciousness), rare severe allergic reactions, lipodystrophy at injection sites.")[Drug: Insulin (Regular / NPH / Analogue formulations)]
Use in Pregnancy: Insulin is the preferred agent for glycemic control in pregnancy when pharmacologic treatment is needed.

(...continued from previous section: "Other glucose-lowering drugs (additive hypoglycemia risk); beta-blockers (may mask hypoglycemia symptoms); corticosteroids and some diuretics can raise glucose and increase insulin requirements.")[Drug: Insulin (Regular / NPH / Analogue formulations)]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Considered compatible with breastfeeding.

(...continued from previous section: "Insulin is the preferred agent for glycemic control in pregnancy when pharmacologic treatment is needed.")[Drug: Insulin (Regular / NPH / Analogue formulations)]
Storage Instructions: Unopened vials/pens: refrigerate at 2–8°C, do not freeze. In-use vial/pen: may be kept at room temperature (below 25–30°C, per specific product label) for up to approximately 28–30 days, protected from direct heat and light, then discarded.

(...continued from previous section: "Considered compatible with breastfeeding.")[Drug: Amlodipine]
Indications / Uses: Hypertension; chronic stable angina; vasospastic (Prinzmetal's) angina.[Drug: Amlodipine]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: initial 5 mg once daily; may be increased to 10 mg once daily based on response (lower starting dose may be used in the elderly or with hepatic impairment).

(...continued from previous section: "Hypertension; chronic stable angina; vasospastic (Prinzmetal's) angina.")[Drug: Amlodipine]
Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to dihydropyridines; use with caution in severe aortic stenosis.

(...continued from previous section: "Adults: initial 5 mg once daily; may be increased to 10 mg once daily based on response (lower starting dose may be used in the elderly or with hepatic impairment).")[Drug: Amlodipine]
Warnings & Precautions: Peripheral edema is a common dose-related effect; caution in severe obstructive coronary artery disease, where initiation or dose increase has rarely been associated with increased angina; symptomatic hypotension possible, particularly in severe aortic stenosis.

(...continued from previous section: "Known hypersensitivity to dihydropyridines; use with caution in severe aortic stenosis.")[Drug: Amlodipine]
Common Side Effects: Peripheral (ankle) edema, flushing, headache, dizziness, palpitations.

(...continued from previous section: "evere obstructive coronary artery disease, where initiation or dose increase has rarely been associated with increased angina; symptomatic hypotension possible, particularly in severe aortic stenosis.")[Drug: Amlodipine]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Significant hypotension, rarely worsening angina/MI on initiation in severe obstructive disease.

(...continued from previous section: "Peripheral (ankle) edema, flushing, headache, dizziness, palpitations.")[Drug: Amlodipine]
Drug Interactions: Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (e.g., certain azole antifungals, macrolides) can increase amlodipine levels; simvastatin dose should be limited to 20 mg/day when co-administered with amlodipine (increased myopathy risk).

(...continued from previous section: "Significant hypotension, rarely worsening angina/MI on initiation in severe obstructive disease.")[Drug: Amlodipine]
Use in Pregnancy: Use only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.

(...continued from previous section: "inhibitors (e.g., certain azole antifungals, macrolides) can increase amlodipine levels; simvastatin dose should be limited to 20 mg/day when co-administered with amlodipine (increased myopathy risk).")[Drug: Amlodipine]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Use with caution; consult a physician.

(...continued from previous section: "Use only if the potential benefit justifies the potential risk to the fetus.")[Drug: Amlodipine]
Storage Instructions: 

(...continued from previous section: "Use with caution; consult a physician.")[Drug: Atorvastatin]
Indications / Uses: Hypercholesterolemia/mixed dyslipidemia; primary and secondary prevention of cardiovascular disease.[Drug: Atorvastatin]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: 10–20 mg once daily initially, adjusted up to a maximum of 80 mg once daily based on lipid response and cardiovascular risk; may be taken at any time of day.

(...continued from previous section: "Hypercholesterolemia/mixed dyslipidemia; primary and secondary prevention of cardiovascular disease.")[Drug: Atorvastatin]
Contraindications: Active liver disease or unexplained persistent elevations in liver enzymes; pregnancy; breastfeeding; known hypersensitivity.

(...continued from previous section: "Adults: 10–20 mg once daily initially, adjusted up to a maximum of 80 mg once daily based on lipid response and cardiovascular risk; may be taken at any time of day.")[Drug: Atorvastatin]
Warnings & Precautions: Risk of myopathy and, rarely, rhabdomyolysis — report unexplained muscle pain, tenderness, or weakness, especially with fever/malaise; monitor liver function as clinically indicated; risk of myopathy increases substantially with concomitant strong CYP3A4 inhibitors or certain other lipid-lowering drugs.

(...continued from previous section: "Active liver disease or unexplained persistent elevations in liver enzymes; pregnancy; breastfeeding; known hypersensitivity.")[Drug: Atorvastatin]
Common Side Effects: Myalgia, headache, GI upset (diarrhea, nausea).

(...continued from previous section: "especially with fever/malaise; monitor liver function as clinically indicated; risk of myopathy increases substantially with concomitant strong CYP3A4 inhibitors or certain other lipid-lowering drugs.")[Drug: Atorvastatin]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Rhabdomyolysis with acute renal failure (rare), hepatotoxicity.

(...continued from previous section: "Myalgia, headache, GI upset (diarrhea, nausea).")[Drug: Atorvastatin]
Drug Interactions: Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (clarithromycin, itraconazole, certain HIV protease inhibitors, grapefruit juice in large amounts) increase levels and myopathy risk; fibrates and gemfibrozil increase myopathy risk; cyclosporine.

(...continued from previous section: "Rhabdomyolysis with acute renal failure (rare), hepatotoxicity.")[Drug: Atorvastatin]
Use in Pregnancy: Contraindicated — discontinue if pregnancy occurs.

(...continued from previous section: "ors (clarithromycin, itraconazole, certain HIV protease inhibitors, grapefruit juice in large amounts) increase levels and myopathy risk; fibrates and gemfibrozil increase myopathy risk; cyclosporine.")[Drug: Atorvastatin]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Contraindicated.

(...continued from previous section: "Contraindicated — discontinue if pregnancy occurs.")[Drug: Atorvastatin]
Storage Instructions: 

(...continued from previous section: "Contraindicated.")[Drug: Losartan]
Indications / Uses: Hypertension; reduction of stroke risk in hypertensive patients with left ventricular hypertrophy; diabetic nephropathy in type 2 diabetes.[Drug: Losartan]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: 50 mg once daily initially, may be increased to 100 mg once daily based on blood pressure response.

(...continued from previous section: "Hypertension; reduction of stroke risk in hypertensive patients with left ventricular hypertrophy; diabetic nephropathy in type 2 diabetes.")[Drug: Losartan]
Contraindications: Pregnancy; bilateral renal artery stenosis (or stenosis in a solitary kidney); known hypersensitivity; concomitant use with aliskiren in patients with diabetes.

(...continued from previous section: "Adults: 50 mg once daily initially, may be increased to 100 mg once daily based on blood pressure response.")[Drug: Losartan]
Warnings & Precautions: Can cause hyperkalemia and renal function changes, particularly in patients with renal impairment, volume depletion, or concurrent use of potassium-sparing agents; symptomatic hypotension possible with volume/salt depletion; rare angioedema.

(...continued from previous section: "Pregnancy; bilateral renal artery stenosis (or stenosis in a solitary kidney); known hypersensitivity; concomitant use with aliskiren in patients with diabetes.")[Drug: Losartan]
Common Side Effects: Dizziness, mild hyperkalemia, fatigue.

(...continued from previous section: " changes, particularly in patients with renal impairment, volume depletion, or concurrent use of potassium-sparing agents; symptomatic hypotension possible with volume/salt depletion; rare angioedema.")[Drug: Losartan]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Angioedema (rare), acute renal impairment, severe hyperkalemia.

(...continued from previous section: "Dizziness, mild hyperkalemia, fatigue.")[Drug: Losartan]
Drug Interactions: Potassium-sparing diuretics and potassium supplements (hyperkalemia risk); NSAIDs (reduced antihypertensive effect, renal risk); lithium (increased levels).

(...continued from previous section: "Angioedema (rare), acute renal impairment, severe hyperkalemia.")[Drug: Losartan]
Use in Pregnancy: Contraindicated, particularly in the second and third trimesters (risk of fetal renal impairment and other injury).

(...continued from previous section: "Potassium-sparing diuretics and potassium supplements (hyperkalemia risk); NSAIDs (reduced antihypertensive effect, renal risk); lithium (increased levels).")[Drug: Losartan]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Not recommended; consult a physician for alternatives.

(...continued from previous section: "Contraindicated, particularly in the second and third trimesters (risk of fetal renal impairment and other injury).")[Drug: Losartan]
Storage Instructions: 

(...continued from previous section: "Not recommended; consult a physician for alternatives.")[Drug: Bisoprolol]
Indications / Uses: Hypertension; chronic stable angina; stable chronic heart failure (as an adjunct to standard therapy).[Drug: Bisoprolol]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Hypertension/angina: 5–10 mg once daily. Heart failure: initiated at a very low dose (e.g., 1.25 mg once daily) and up-titrated slowly under medical supervision.

(...continued from previous section: "Hypertension; chronic stable angina; stable chronic heart failure (as an adjunct to standard therapy).")[Drug: Bisoprolol]
Contraindications: Severe bradycardia; second- or third-degree AV block without a pacemaker; decompensated (acute) heart failure; cardiogenic shock; severe/unstable asthma or bronchospastic disease.

(...continued from previous section: "Hypertension/angina: 5–10 mg once daily. Heart failure: initiated at a very low dose (e.g., 1.25 mg once daily) and up-titrated slowly under medical supervision.")[Drug: Bisoprolol]
Warnings & Precautions: Do not stop abruptly — taper gradually to avoid rebound hypertension, angina, or arrhythmia; can mask the tachycardia associated with hypoglycemia in diabetic patients; use with caution in well-controlled asthma/COPD.

(...continued from previous section: "Severe bradycardia; second- or third-degree AV block without a pacemaker; decompensated (acute) heart failure; cardiogenic shock; severe/unstable asthma or bronchospastic disease.")[Drug: Bisoprolol]
Common Side Effects: Fatigue, bradycardia, cold extremities, dizziness.

(...continued from previous section: "tly — taper gradually to avoid rebound hypertension, angina, or arrhythmia; can mask the tachycardia associated with hypoglycemia in diabetic patients; use with caution in well-controlled asthma/COPD.")[Drug: Bisoprolol]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Severe bradycardia, heart block, bronchospasm in susceptible patients.

(...continued from previous section: "Fatigue, bradycardia, cold extremities, dizziness.")[Drug: Bisoprolol]
Drug Interactions: Other heart-rate-lowering drugs (verapamil, diltiazem — risk of severe bradycardia/AV block); clonidine (risk of rebound hypertension if both withdrawn together); insulin/oral antidiabetics (masked hypoglycemia symptoms).

(...continued from previous section: "Severe bradycardia, heart block, bronchospasm in susceptible patients.")[Drug: Bisoprolol]
Use in Pregnancy: Use with caution; monitor fetal growth if used, particularly in the third trimester.

(...continued from previous section: "ring drugs (verapamil, diltiazem — risk of severe bradycardia/AV block); clonidine (risk of rebound hypertension if both withdrawn together); insulin/oral antidiabetics (masked hypoglycemia symptoms).")[Drug: Bisoprolol]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Use with caution; consult a physician.

(...continued from previous section: "Use with caution; monitor fetal growth if used, particularly in the third trimester.")[Drug: Bisoprolol]
