(...continued from previous section: "Use with caution; may reduce milk volume; consult a physician.")[Drug: Omeprazole]
Indications / Uses: Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori eradication (in combination with antibiotics), and pathological hypersecretory conditions.[Drug: Omeprazole]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: 20–40 mg once daily before breakfast, for a duration dependent on indication.

(...continued from previous section: "Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori eradication (in combination with antibiotics), and pathological hypersecretory conditions.")[Drug: Omeprazole]
Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to PPIs.

(...continued from previous section: "Adults: 20–40 mg once daily before breakfast, for a duration dependent on indication.")[Drug: Omeprazole]
Warnings & Precautions: Long-term use is associated with increased risk of vitamin B12 deficiency, hypomagnesemia, bone fracture with high-dose/long-term use, and Clostridioides difficile-associated diarrhea; PPIs can mask symptoms of gastric malignancy and delay diagnosis — evaluate alarm symptoms before starting long-term therapy.

(...continued from previous section: "Known hypersensitivity to PPIs.")[Drug: Omeprazole]
Common Side Effects: Headache, abdominal pain, diarrhea or constipation.

(...continued from previous section: "high-dose/long-term use, and Clostridioides difficile-associated diarrhea; PPIs can mask symptoms of gastric malignancy and delay diagnosis — evaluate alarm symptoms before starting long-term therapy.")[Drug: Omeprazole]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Severe hypomagnesemia, C. difficile colitis, rare hepatic effects, increased fracture risk with prolonged high-dose use.

(...continued from previous section: "Headache, abdominal pain, diarrhea or constipation.")[Drug: Omeprazole]
Drug Interactions: Significantly reduces the antiplatelet effect of clopidogrel (concomitant use generally avoided); reduces absorption of drugs requiring acidic gastric pH (e.g., ketoconazole, certain iron salts); can increase digoxin levels.

(...continued from previous section: "Severe hypomagnesemia, C. difficile colitis, rare hepatic effects, increased fracture risk with prolonged high-dose use.")[Drug: Omeprazole]
Use in Pregnancy: Generally considered low risk when clinically indicated.

(...continued from previous section: "e antiplatelet effect of clopidogrel (concomitant use generally avoided); reduces absorption of drugs requiring acidic gastric pH (e.g., ketoconazole, certain iron salts); can increase digoxin levels.")[Drug: Omeprazole]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Generally considered compatible; consult a physician.

(...continued from previous section: "Generally considered low risk when clinically indicated.")[Drug: Omeprazole]
Storage Instructions: Room temperature, protect from moisture; keep in original container (light-sensitive).

(...continued from previous section: "Generally considered compatible; consult a physician.")[Drug: Domperidone]
Indications / Uses: Relief of symptoms of nausea, vomiting, and dyspepsia associated with delayed gastric emptying.[Drug: Domperidone]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: 10 mg up to 3 times daily before meals; use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration necessary (generally not beyond 1 week without medical review).

(...continued from previous section: "Relief of symptoms of nausea, vomiting, and dyspepsia associated with delayed gastric emptying.")[Drug: Domperidone]
Contraindications: Known cardiac conduction abnormalities or significant structural heart disease; moderate to severe hepatic impairment; concomitant use with QT-prolonging drugs or strong CYP3A4 inhibitors; prolactin-dependent conditions (e.g., certain pituitary tumors).

(...continued from previous section: "Adults: 10 mg up to 3 times daily before meals; use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration necessary (generally not beyond 1 week without medical review).")[Drug: Domperidone]
Warnings & Precautions: Associated with a small increased risk of serious cardiac arrhythmia and sudden cardiac death, particularly at higher doses, in patients over 60 years, or with cardiac risk factors — use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration and avoid in those with significant cardiac risk.

(...continued from previous section: " structural heart disease; moderate to severe hepatic impairment; concomitant use with QT-prolonging drugs or strong CYP3A4 inhibitors; prolactin-dependent conditions (e.g., certain pituitary tumors).")[Drug: Domperidone]
Common Side Effects: Dry mouth, headache, mild abdominal cramping.

(...continued from previous section: "death, particularly at higher doses, in patients over 60 years, or with cardiac risk factors — use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration and avoid in those with significant cardiac risk.")[Drug: Domperidone]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: QT prolongation, cardiac arrhythmia (rare but serious).

(...continued from previous section: "Dry mouth, headache, mild abdominal cramping.")[Drug: Domperidone]
Drug Interactions: Other QT-prolonging drugs; strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (certain azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics) — avoid combination.

(...continued from previous section: "QT prolongation, cardiac arrhythmia (rare but serious).")[Drug: Domperidone]
Use in Pregnancy: Use only if clearly needed and under medical guidance.

(...continued from previous section: "Other QT-prolonging drugs; strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (certain azole antifungals, macrolide antibiotics) — avoid combination.")[Drug: Domperidone]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Consult a physician; used with caution.

(...continued from previous section: "Use only if clearly needed and under medical guidance.")[Drug: Domperidone]
Storage Instructions: Room temperature, protect from light.

(...continued from previous section: "Consult a physician; used with caution.")[Drug: Loperamide]
Indications / Uses: Symptomatic relief of acute non-specific diarrhea and chronic diarrhea associated with inflammatory bowel disease (under medical guidance).[Drug: Loperamide]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: 4 mg initially, then 2 mg after each loose stool; do not exceed the maximum daily dose stated on the product label (OTC use is typically limited to a few days).

(...continued from previous section: "Symptomatic relief of acute non-specific diarrhea and chronic diarrhea associated with inflammatory bowel disease (under medical guidance).")[Drug: Loperamide]
Contraindications: Diarrhea with high fever or blood in the stool (possible invasive infection — evaluate before use); acute ulcerative colitis; pseudomembranous colitis associated with antibiotic use; children below the age specified on the product label.

(...continued from previous section: "Adults: 4 mg initially, then 2 mg after each loose stool; do not exceed the maximum daily dose stated on the product label (OTC use is typically limited to a few days).")[Drug: Loperamide]
Warnings & Precautions: Should not be used as a substitute for fluid and electrolyte replacement; discontinue and seek medical care if diarrhea does not improve within 48 hours, or if fever/blood in stool develops; supratherapeutic or misuse doses have been associated with serious cardiac arrhythmias (QT prolongation).

(...continued from previous section: "the stool (possible invasive infection — evaluate before use); acute ulcerative colitis; pseudomembranous colitis associated with antibiotic use; children below the age specified on the product label.")[Drug: Loperamide]
Common Side Effects: Constipation, abdominal cramping, mild dizziness.

(...continued from previous section: "edical care if diarrhea does not improve within 48 hours, or if fever/blood in stool develops; supratherapeutic or misuse doses have been associated with serious cardiac arrhythmias (QT prolongation).")[Drug: Loperamide]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Toxic megacolon (in colitis), cardiac arrhythmia with overdose/misuse.

(...continued from previous section: "Constipation, abdominal cramping, mild dizziness.")[Drug: Loperamide]
Drug Interactions: Drugs that inhibit CYP3A4/P-glycoprotein can increase loperamide levels and cardiac risk (e.g., quinidine); use caution with other QT-prolonging drugs.

(...continued from previous section: "Toxic megacolon (in colitis), cardiac arrhythmia with overdose/misuse.")[Drug: Loperamide]
Use in Pregnancy: Use with caution and only if clearly needed.

(...continued from previous section: "Drugs that inhibit CYP3A4/P-glycoprotein can increase loperamide levels and cardiac risk (e.g., quinidine); use caution with other QT-prolonging drugs.")[Drug: Loperamide]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Use with caution; consult a physician.

(...continued from previous section: "Use with caution and only if clearly needed.")[Drug: Loperamide]
