(...continued from previous section: "Generally considered compatible; monitor infant for drowsiness.")[Drug: Loratadine]
Indications / Uses: Allergic rhinitis, chronic idiopathic urticaria.[Drug: Loratadine]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: 10 mg once daily.

(...continued from previous section: "Allergic rhinitis, chronic idiopathic urticaria.")[Drug: Loratadine]
Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to loratadine or its components.

(...continued from previous section: "Adults: 10 mg once daily.")[Drug: Loratadine]
Warnings & Precautions: Dose adjustment (e.g., alternate-day dosing) is recommended in significant hepatic impairment; generally non-sedating at recommended doses, but individual sensitivity varies.

(...continued from previous section: "Known hypersensitivity to loratadine or its components.")[Drug: Loratadine]
Common Side Effects: Headache, fatigue, dry mouth.

(...continued from previous section: "Dose adjustment (e.g., alternate-day dosing) is recommended in significant hepatic impairment; generally non-sedating at recommended doses, but individual sensitivity varies.")[Drug: Loratadine]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Rare hypersensitivity reactions.

(...continued from previous section: "Headache, fatigue, dry mouth.")[Drug: Loratadine]
Drug Interactions: Strong CYP3A4/CYP2D6 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, erythromycin) may increase loratadine plasma levels — generally without significant clinical effect at standard doses, but caution is advised.

(...continued from previous section: "Rare hypersensitivity reactions.")[Drug: Loratadine]
Use in Pregnancy: Generally considered to have a low risk profile; use if clinically needed.

(...continued from previous section: "Strong CYP3A4/CYP2D6 inhibitors (e.g., ketoconazole, erythromycin) may increase loratadine plasma levels — generally without significant clinical effect at standard doses, but caution is advised.")[Drug: Loratadine]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Generally considered compatible.

(...continued from previous section: "Generally considered to have a low risk profile; use if clinically needed.")[Drug: Loratadine]
