(...continued from previous section: "Generally considered compatible.")[Drug: Salbutamol (Albuterol)]
Indications / Uses: Relief of acute bronchospasm in asthma and COPD; prevention of exercise-induced bronchospasm.[Drug: Salbutamol (Albuterol)]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Inhaler: 1–2 puffs (100–200 mcg) as needed for symptom relief, up to the maximum stated on the product label. Nebulizer: 2.5–5 mg as needed, diluted per protocol.

(...continued from previous section: "Relief of acute bronchospasm in asthma and COPD; prevention of exercise-induced bronchospasm.")[Drug: Salbutamol (Albuterol)]
Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to salbutamol or its excipients.

(...continued from previous section: "Inhaler: 1–2 puffs (100–200 mcg) as needed for symptom relief, up to the maximum stated on the product label. Nebulizer: 2.5–5 mg as needed, diluted per protocol.")[Drug: Salbutamol (Albuterol)]
Warnings & Precautions: Increasing or regular need for a short-acting bronchodilator indicates poorly controlled asthma and requires medical reassessment; paradoxical bronchospasm has rarely been reported (discontinue and seek alternative therapy if it occurs); cardiovascular effects (tachycardia, tremor) more likely with overuse or in patients with cardiac disease or hyperthyroidism.

(...continued from previous section: "Known hypersensitivity to salbutamol or its excipients.")[Drug: Salbutamol (Albuterol)]
Common Side Effects: Tremor, tachycardia, nervousness, headache.

(...continued from previous section: "ely been reported (discontinue and seek alternative therapy if it occurs); cardiovascular effects (tachycardia, tremor) more likely with overuse or in patients with cardiac disease or hyperthyroidism.")[Drug: Salbutamol (Albuterol)]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Paradoxical bronchospasm, significant cardiac arrhythmia (uncommon), hypokalemia with high/frequent doses (especially with concurrent diuretics or in severe asthma exacerbation).

(...continued from previous section: "Tremor, tachycardia, nervousness, headache.")[Drug: Salbutamol (Albuterol)]
Drug Interactions: Non-selective beta-blockers can antagonize the bronchodilator effect and are generally avoided in patients with asthma; other sympathomimetic drugs may have additive cardiovascular effects; diuretics may worsen salbutamol-associated hypokalemia.

(...continued from previous section: "Paradoxical bronchospasm, significant cardiac arrhythmia (uncommon), hypokalemia with high/frequent doses (especially with concurrent diuretics or in severe asthma exacerbation).")[Drug: Salbutamol (Albuterol)]
Use in Pregnancy: Commonly used when needed for asthma control in pregnancy; uncontrolled asthma poses greater risk than appropriate treatment.

(...continued from previous section: "e bronchodilator effect and are generally avoided in patients with asthma; other sympathomimetic drugs may have additive cardiovascular effects; diuretics may worsen salbutamol-associated hypokalemia.")[Drug: Salbutamol (Albuterol)]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Generally considered compatible.

(...continued from previous section: "Commonly used when needed for asthma control in pregnancy; uncontrolled asthma poses greater risk than appropriate treatment.")[Drug: Salbutamol (Albuterol)]
Storage Instructions: Store below 25–30°C; avoid exposing the canister to heat, freezing, or direct sunlight; do not puncture or incinerate.

(...continued from previous section: "Generally considered compatible.")[Drug: Prednisolone]
Indications / Uses: Wide range of inflammatory, allergic, and autoimmune conditions (e.g., asthma exacerbations, severe allergic reactions, certain rheumatologic and dermatologic conditions), used as directed by a physician.[Drug: Prednisolone]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Highly variable by indication and severity, commonly in the range of 5–60 mg/day; short courses may be stopped directly, while longer courses require a gradual taper as directed by the prescriber.

(...continued from previous section: " range of inflammatory, allergic, and autoimmune conditions (e.g., asthma exacerbations, severe allergic reactions, certain rheumatologic and dermatologic conditions), used as directed by a physician.")[Drug: Prednisolone]
Contraindications: Systemic fungal infection; known hypersensitivity; live vaccines should generally be avoided during high-dose or prolonged systemic therapy.

(...continued from previous section: "Highly variable by indication and severity, commonly in the range of 5–60 mg/day; short courses may be stopped directly, while longer courses require a gradual taper as directed by the prescriber.")[Drug: Prednisolone]
Warnings & Precautions: Abrupt discontinuation after prolonged use can cause adrenal insufficiency — taper gradually under medical supervision; increases susceptibility to infection and can mask signs of infection; long-term use is associated with hyperglycemia, osteoporosis, adrenal suppression, mood/behavioral changes, and increased GI ulceration risk (particularly with concurrent NSAID use).

(...continued from previous section: "Systemic fungal infection; known hypersensitivity; live vaccines should generally be avoided during high-dose or prolonged systemic therapy.")[Drug: Prednisolone]
Common Side Effects: Increased appetite, weight gain, insomnia, mood changes, indigestion.

(...continued from previous section: "gns of infection; long-term use is associated with hyperglycemia, osteoporosis, adrenal suppression, mood/behavioral changes, and increased GI ulceration risk (particularly with concurrent NSAID use).")[Drug: Prednisolone]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Adrenal insufficiency (on withdrawal), severe infections, GI bleeding/ulceration, osteoporosis/fractures, hyperglycemia, psychiatric disturbances.

(...continued from previous section: "Increased appetite, weight gain, insomnia, mood changes, indigestion.")[Drug: Prednisolone]
Drug Interactions: NSAIDs (increased GI bleeding risk); live vaccines (avoid during significant immunosuppression); antidiabetic drugs (reduced effect, higher glucose); diuretics (increased hypokalemia risk).

(...continued from previous section: "Adrenal insufficiency (on withdrawal), severe infections, GI bleeding/ulceration, osteoporosis/fractures, hyperglycemia, psychiatric disturbances.")[Drug: Prednisolone]
Use in Pregnancy: Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration when clinically necessary.

(...continued from previous section: "NSAIDs (increased GI bleeding risk); live vaccines (avoid during significant immunosuppression); antidiabetic drugs (reduced effect, higher glucose); diuretics (increased hypokalemia risk).")[Drug: Prednisolone]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Generally considered compatible at low-to-moderate doses; consult a physician for high-dose or prolonged use.

(...continued from previous section: "Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration when clinically necessary.")[Drug: Prednisolone]
Storage Instructions: 

(...continued from previous section: "Generally considered compatible at low-to-moderate doses; consult a physician for high-dose or prolonged use.")[Drug: Levothyroxine]
Indications / Uses: Hypothyroidism (replacement therapy); TSH suppression in specific thyroid conditions as directed by a physician.[Drug: Levothyroxine]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Individualized based on age, weight, cardiac status, and TSH levels; typically started at a low dose in the elderly or those with cardiac disease and titrated gradually. Take on an empty stomach, 30–60 minutes before breakfast, at a consistent time each day.

(...continued from previous section: "Hypothyroidism (replacement therapy); TSH suppression in specific thyroid conditions as directed by a physician.")[Drug: Levothyroxine]
Contraindications: Untreated adrenal insufficiency; untreated thyrotoxicosis; acute, uncorrected myocardial infarction; known hypersensitivity to the formulation.

(...continued from previous section: "SH levels; typically started at a low dose in the elderly or those with cardiac disease and titrated gradually. Take on an empty stomach, 30–60 minutes before breakfast, at a consistent time each day.")[Drug: Levothyroxine]
Warnings & Precautions: Overtreatment can cause symptoms of thyrotoxicosis, cardiac arrhythmia (especially in the elderly or those with cardiac disease), and accelerated bone loss with long-term excess dosing; many drugs and foods interfere with absorption — administer consistently relative to meals and other medications.

(...continued from previous section: "Untreated adrenal insufficiency; untreated thyrotoxicosis; acute, uncorrected myocardial infarction; known hypersensitivity to the formulation.")[Drug: Levothyroxine]
Common Side Effects: At correct dose, generally no adverse effects; overdose symptoms include palpitations, weight loss, tremor, insomnia, heat intolerance.

(...continued from previous section: "r those with cardiac disease), and accelerated bone loss with long-term excess dosing; many drugs and foods interfere with absorption — administer consistently relative to meals and other medications.")[Drug: Levothyroxine]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Cardiac arrhythmia, angina exacerbation, osteoporosis with chronic overtreatment.

(...continued from previous section: "At correct dose, generally no adverse effects; overdose symptoms include palpitations, weight loss, tremor, insomnia, heat intolerance.")[Drug: Levothyroxine]
Drug Interactions: Calcium and iron supplements and antacids reduce absorption — separate administration by at least 4 hours; PPIs may reduce absorption; increases the anticoagulant effect of warfarin (monitor INR); estrogen therapy may alter dosing requirements.

(...continued from previous section: "Cardiac arrhythmia, angina exacerbation, osteoporosis with chronic overtreatment.")[Drug: Levothyroxine]
Use in Pregnancy: Treatment is continued and the dose is often increased during pregnancy under close monitoring, as maternal hypothyroidism can affect fetal development.

(...continued from previous section: "duce absorption — separate administration by at least 4 hours; PPIs may reduce absorption; increases the anticoagulant effect of warfarin (monitor INR); estrogen therapy may alter dosing requirements.")[Drug: Levothyroxine]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Considered compatible with breastfeeding; adequate maternal thyroid hormone is important during lactation.

(...continued from previous section: "Treatment is continued and the dose is often increased during pregnancy under close monitoring, as maternal hypothyroidism can affect fetal development.")[Drug: Levothyroxine]
Storage Instructions: Room temperature, protect from light and moisture.

(...continued from previous section: "Considered compatible with breastfeeding; adequate maternal thyroid hormone is important during lactation.")[Drug: Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol)]
Indications / Uses: Prevention and treatment of vitamin D deficiency; prevention/treatment of rickets and osteomalacia; adjunct in the management of osteoporosis.[Drug: Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol)]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Highly variable: deficiency is often treated with a high stat/loading dose (e.g., 200,000 IU once, or a short loading course) followed by maintenance dosing (commonly 800–2000 IU/day); dosing should follow measured vitamin D levels and medical guidance.

(...continued from previous section: "Prevention and treatment of vitamin D deficiency; prevention/treatment of rickets and osteomalacia; adjunct in the management of osteoporosis.")[Drug: Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol)]
Contraindications: Hypercalcemia; evidence of vitamin D toxicity; known hypersensitivity to the formulation.

(...continued from previous section: "igh stat/loading dose (e.g., 200,000 IU once, or a short loading course) followed by maintenance dosing (commonly 800–2000 IU/day); dosing should follow measured vitamin D levels and medical guidance.")[Drug: Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol)]
Warnings & Precautions: Excessive or inappropriate dosing can cause hypercalcemia and hypervitaminosis D — monitor serum calcium with prolonged high-dose regimens or in patients with renal impairment, sarcoidosis, or other conditions affecting calcium metabolism.

(...continued from previous section: "Hypercalcemia; evidence of vitamin D toxicity; known hypersensitivity to the formulation.")[Drug: Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol)]
Common Side Effects: Generally well tolerated at recommended doses.

(...continued from previous section: "ause hypercalcemia and hypervitaminosis D — monitor serum calcium with prolonged high-dose regimens or in patients with renal impairment, sarcoidosis, or other conditions affecting calcium metabolism.")[Drug: Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol)]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Hypercalcemia (nausea, weakness, constipation, renal impairment, arrhythmia) with excessive dosing.

(...continued from previous section: "Generally well tolerated at recommended doses.")[Drug: Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol)]
Drug Interactions: Thiazide diuretics increase the risk of hypercalcemia when combined with vitamin D and calcium; corticosteroids can reduce vitamin D's calcium-absorption effect; digoxin toxicity risk is increased if hypercalcemia develops.

(...continued from previous section: "Hypercalcemia (nausea, weakness, constipation, renal impairment, arrhythmia) with excessive dosing.")[Drug: Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol)]
Use in Pregnancy: Recommended at appropriate doses for maternal and fetal bone health; avoid excessive/unsupervised high-dose regimens.

(...continued from previous section: "ease the risk of hypercalcemia when combined with vitamin D and calcium; corticosteroids can reduce vitamin D's calcium-absorption effect; digoxin toxicity risk is increased if hypercalcemia develops.")[Drug: Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol)]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Considered safe and often recommended at appropriate doses.

(...continued from previous section: "Recommended at appropriate doses for maternal and fetal bone health; avoid excessive/unsupervised high-dose regimens.")[Drug: Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol)]
Storage Instructions: Room temperature, protect from light.

(...continued from previous section: "Considered safe and often recommended at appropriate doses.")[Drug: Ferrous Sulfate / Iron Supplements]
Indications / Uses: Prevention and treatment of iron-deficiency anemia; iron supplementation during pregnancy.[Drug: Ferrous Sulfate / Iron Supplements]
Adult Dosage & Administration: Adults: typically 60–120 mg elemental iron per day, in single or divided doses; take on an empty stomach for best absorption if tolerated, or with a small amount of food if GI upset occurs (avoid taking with tea, coffee, dairy, or antacids, which reduce absorption).

(...continued from previous section: "Prevention and treatment of iron-deficiency anemia; iron supplementation during pregnancy.")[Drug: Ferrous Sulfate / Iron Supplements]
Contraindications: Hemochromatosis or other iron-overload conditions; hemolytic anemia not due to iron deficiency; known hypersensitivity.

(...continued from previous section: "ivided doses; take on an empty stomach for best absorption if tolerated, or with a small amount of food if GI upset occurs (avoid taking with tea, coffee, dairy, or antacids, which reduce absorption).")[Drug: Ferrous Sulfate / Iron Supplements]
Warnings & Precautions: GI side effects are common and dose-related; iron products are a leading cause of accidental poisoning deaths in young children — store tightly closed and strictly out of children's reach; liquid formulations can stain teeth (use a straw and rinse the mouth after dosing).

(...continued from previous section: "Hemochromatosis or other iron-overload conditions; hemolytic anemia not due to iron deficiency; known hypersensitivity.")[Drug: Ferrous Sulfate / Iron Supplements]
Common Side Effects: Constipation, dark/black stools (expected, harmless), nausea, abdominal discomfort.

(...continued from previous section: " cause of accidental poisoning deaths in young children — store tightly closed and strictly out of children's reach; liquid formulations can stain teeth (use a straw and rinse the mouth after dosing).")[Drug: Ferrous Sulfate / Iron Supplements]
Serious Side Effects / Seek Medical Help: Iron overdose toxicity — potentially fatal in children even with a relatively small number of tablets; GI ulceration/corrosive injury with very high doses.

(...continued from previous section: "Constipation, dark/black stools (expected, harmless), nausea, abdominal discomfort.")[Drug: Ferrous Sulfate / Iron Supplements]
Drug Interactions: Reduces absorption of levothyroxine, tetracyclines, and fluoroquinolones when taken together — separate dosing by at least 2 hours; antacids and PPIs reduce iron absorption; vitamin C enhances iron absorption.

(...continued from previous section: "Iron overdose toxicity — potentially fatal in children even with a relatively small number of tablets; GI ulceration/corrosive injury with very high doses.")[Drug: Ferrous Sulfate / Iron Supplements]
Use in Pregnancy: Commonly recommended as a supplement during pregnancy to prevent or treat deficiency, per antenatal guidance.

(...continued from previous section: "bsorption of levothyroxine, tetracyclines, and fluoroquinolones when taken together — separate dosing by at least 2 hours; antacids and PPIs reduce iron absorption; vitamin C enhances iron absorption.")[Drug: Ferrous Sulfate / Iron Supplements]
Use in Breastfeeding (Lactation): Considered safe and often continued during breastfeeding if indicated.

(...continued from previous section: "Commonly recommended as a supplement during pregnancy to prevent or treat deficiency, per antenatal guidance.")[Drug: Ferrous Sulfate / Iron Supplements]
Storage Instructions: Room temperature, protect from moisture, keep container tightly closed. KEEP OUT OF REACH OF CHILDREN — accidental ingestion is a medical emergency.

(...continued from previous section: "Considered safe and often continued during breastfeeding if indicated.")